\title{Discrete Math learning goals}
\date{2026-02-23T20:17:52Z}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{For each [learning goal](https://hendrix-cs.github.io/math240/#learning-goals-1) we have covered below, you will find links to relevant sections of the lecture notes.}

\subtree{
  \title{Propositional Logic + Proofs}
    \ul{
      \li{L1: I can make truth tables for propositional logic expressions involving TRUE, FALSE, AND, OR, NOT, IMPLIES, IFF, and variables.
      \ul{
        \li{[[002Y]]}
        \li{[[0031]]}
        \li{[Example truth table](0043)}
      }
      }
        \li{L2: [I can use algebraic laws to rewrite and simplify, or prove the equivalence of, formulas of propositional logic.](0045)}
      \li{L3: I can evaluate the truth or falsity of propositions with quantifiers.
      \ul{
        \li{[[004G]]}
        \li{[[004O]]}
        \li{[[0050]]}
      }
      }
        \li{L4: [I can use De Morgan laws to simplify negations of propositions with quantifiers, AND, and OR](004Y).}
        \li{L5: [I can formalize English statements as propositional logic formulas, making appropriate use of logical connectives and nested quantifiers](004R).}
        \li{L6: [I can express quantified statements in equivalent ways with different domains](0051).}
        \li{P1: [I can write an appropriate proof outline for a given propositional logic formula](0057).}
}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Set Theory}
  \ul{
    \li{S1: [I can state the definitions, and determine membership, of standard sets such as N, Z, Q, and R](006J).}
    \li{S2: I can evaluate and construct sets using union, intersection, difference, and complement of sets, and sets defined via set builder notation.
      \ul{
        \li{[[0068]]}
        \li{[[0069]]}
        \li{[[0071]]}
      }
    }
    \li{S3: I can list all the elements in a power set, Cartesian product, or disjoint union of sets, or count them without listing them all.
    \ul{
      \li{[[0072]]}
      \li{[[0077]]}
      \li{[[007B]]}
    }
    }
    \li{P2: I can reproduce proofs about sets, set operations, and the subset relation.
      \ul{
        \li{[[006Q]]}
        \li{[[0082]]}
      }
    }
  }
}

